        'title' => 'Twenty-eight years on a warehouse floor, replaced by a robot with no smoke breaks',
        'body' => <<<'STORY'
I ran operations at a logistics company for twenty-eight years, starting as a picker on the floor and working my way up to operations manager. By the end I had four warehouses under me, forty-some staff, and a back that reminded me every morning exactly how long I'd been doing this.

The job was never really about boxes. It was about people. Knowing which forklift driver needed a break before he said so. Knowing which new hire was about to quit because his shoes hurt and he was too proud to ask for the safety boots in his size. I used to walk the floor at six in the morning before anyone else showed up, just to feel the place before the noise started. That quiet hour was mine.

About four years ago the company brought in an automated sorting system, the kind with little robots that glide under shelving units and bring the whole shelf to the picker instead of the picker walking to the shelf. I'll admit it, the first week I watched those things work I was genuinely impressed. No coffee breaks, no arguments about who left the pallet jack in the wrong bay, no forgetting where they put something. They just moved, all night if you let them, silent as ghosts.

The trouble is a robot doesn't know that Thursday is when the new mother on the evening shift needs to leave twenty minutes early to catch the last bus that connects to her babysitter. A robot doesn't notice that the temp who's been quiet all week just found out his father's sick back home. Half my job for almost three decades was reading a warehouse floor like a room full of people, not a grid of shelving. That skill doesn't show up on an efficiency report, so nobody thought to ask if the company still needed it.

They didn't fire me outright. It was gentler than that, and somehow that made it worse. My role got "restructured" into something called a transition coordinator, which meant training the very automation that was making my old job disappear, then watching my headcount shrink every quarter until there wasn't really a floor to manage anymore, just software dashboards and a skeleton crew of technicians who fixed robots instead of people who fixed problems.

I'll say this for the young engineer who ran the automation rollout, he was decent about it, always asked my opinion even though I suspect he already knew the answer before he asked. Near the end he told me the system had cut fulfillment errors by sixty percent. I told him that's great, genuinely, but ask it sometime how many marriages it saved by giving a night-shift father a lighter week when his kid was in the hospital. He didn't have an answer for that, and I didn't expect one.

I took the severance, which was fair, more than fair honestly, and I've spent the last year and a half doing consulting for smaller outfits that can't afford full automation yet, teaching them the same floor-reading tricks nobody wanted from me at the big place anymore. It pays less. It also feels like the last honest use of everything I learned.

My grandson asked me once if I was mad at the robots. I told him no, I'm not mad at a machine for doing what it was built to do. I'm just glad I got twenty-eight years of doing a job where the most important tool I carried was my own attention, before the industry decided attention could be automated too. Some mornings I still wake up at five thirty out of habit, ready to walk a floor that doesn't need me walking it anymore. I usually just make coffee instead and let the quiet hour be mine again, for no reason at all.
