Thirty-two years as a site supervisor for residential and commercial interior fit-outs, and I can tell you almost to the week when the trade started changing under me, because it lined up almost exactly with when the company I worked for got bought by a bigger regional outfit chasing volume.

I came up the old way, apprenticed under a supervisor who used to say a building doesn't lie to you, it just waits until you're not looking and then it tells everyone else the truth. I believed that for three decades and I still believe it. My whole career was built on the boring, unglamorous parts nobody photographs for a portfolio, the vapor barriers installed correctly, the fire-rated materials actually rated for fire, the electrical work inspected properly before the drywall goes up and hides it forever.

The last five years, after the buyout, the pressure to compress timelines got severe in a way I hadn't experienced before. Projects that used to get six weeks got scheduled for four, with the same scope, because sales had promised faster turnaround to win the contract against competitors doing the same thing. I understood the business pressure. I'm not naive about margins. But there's a difference between working efficiently and skipping the parts of the process that exist specifically because somebody, somewhere, learned the hard way why they matter.

The incident that ended it for me was smaller than people probably expect. We were finishing a commercial office fit-out, and the schedule had slipped because of a supplier delay that wasn't our fault. Rather than push the deadline, the new project director wanted to close up a section of wall before the required inspection on some electrical work, planning to schedule the inspector after the fact and just deal with any issues by opening the wall back up later if needed. He framed it as a minor administrative shortcut. I've been doing this thirty-two years. I know exactly what it actually is.

I refused to sign off on it. Not loudly, not dramatically, I just said no, and explained why, the same way I'd explained things to twenty project directors before him. This one didn't like being told no by someone he considered old-fashioned. He went over my head, got a more junior supervisor to authorize it instead, and the wall went up. Nothing went wrong, as it happens, this time. But I'd spent three decades building a reputation on the idea that my signature on a site sheet meant something, and watching it get worked around like an inconvenience told me everything about where the company was headed.

I finished that project because I don't leave a job half-built, whatever else is going on. Then I gave notice. My director actually seemed surprised, said he thought I'd understand the business realities of a competitive market. I told him I understood the realities fine, I just wasn't willing to spend the last years of my career being the guy remembered for the wall that hid something it shouldn't have.

There's a bittersweet part to this I don't fully know how to explain to people outside the trade. I'm not angry at the industry for changing, competition does that to every business eventually, and I don't think everyone at that company was cutting corners, most of the crews I worked with till the end were as careful as I'd always been. I'm just old enough now to have watched an entire way of doing careful, patient work become something companies now describe as inefficient. That's a strange thing to make peace with, mourning a value nobody else in the room seems to have noticed disappearing.

These days I do independent inspections and consulting for homeowners doing renovations, checking other people's work before they close up walls of their own. It's quieter, it's slower, and every single client actually wants to hear what I find, good or bad. That's really all I ever wanted from the job in the first place.
