I started teaching when the standard classroom had a chalkboard, a cassette player for listening exercises, and photocopied worksheets so blurry the students used to joke they were learning a secret dialect only visible under moonlight. I built my whole teaching identity around the room itself, reading which student was too shy to speak up and needed to be called on gently rather than put on the spot, knowing which grammar point always confused speakers of certain languages and adjusting my explanation before they even asked.

Over the decades I watched the school modernize piece by piece, interactive whiteboards, then language learning software in the computer lab, then eventually students showing up with apps on their phones that could correct their pronunciation better than my aging ears could in a noisy classroom of thirty. I want to be honest, my first reaction to each of these was mild resentment, the specific kind of resentment a craftsman feels watching a machine learn to do the delicate part of the job they were proudest of.

But something shifted for me around five years ago that I didn't expect. A former student, grown now with kids of his own, came back to visit and told me the app on his phone had taught him excellent grammar, but it was the six months he spent in my class stumbling through conversation practice, getting corrected kindly in front of his classmates, laughing at his own mistakes instead of hiding them, that actually made him confident enough to use the language out loud with strangers. The app taught him the rules. The classroom taught him not to be afraid.

That conversation reframed the whole ending of my career for me. I stopped seeing the software as competition and started treating it as the thing that could finally take the repetitive drilling off my plate, the vocabulary quizzes, the pronunciation grading, the parts of teaching that were necessary but never where the real magic happened. I spent my last few years leaning harder into exactly the parts an app cannot do, running debate clubs, coaching painfully shy adult students through their first real conversations, staying twenty minutes late because someone finally worked up the nerve to ask the question they'd been sitting on for a month.

When I decided to retire, it wasn't because the industry pushed me out, and that's rare enough in stories like this that I want to say it plainly. I chose the timing. I trained my replacement, a sharp woman in her thirties who grew up with these apps as a normal part of learning rather than an intrusion, and I watched her build lesson plans that blended the software and the classroom far more gracefully than I ever managed to in my more resistant years. Handing it to her felt less like losing something and more like finally putting down a bag I'd been carrying a long time, knowing the next person would carry it well.

My leaving party, the one with the cake, had almost twenty former students there, some from thirty years back, grey-haired themselves now. One of them stood up and said, in careful English I recognized as exactly the sentence structure I used to drill into that particular group, that I had taught her the language but more importantly taught her she was allowed to sound foolish while learning it. I cried a little, which is not very British of me, but at that age you stop pretending you won't.

I still tutor two mornings a week, just for the joy of it, no app involved, just a table, two cups of tea, and whoever's brave enough to keep talking even when they get it wrong.
